published.resolution_text:
WHEREAS the City is responsible for regulating building construction and licensing businesses under the Community Charter, and for many residents, builders and business owners, the permitting and licensing process is among their most direct interactions with City Hall;
WHEREAS timely, predictable and transparent approval processes are essential to providing excellent public service, reducing costs, carrying charges and uncertainty for applicants, and supporting the delivery of housing, commercial space and employment opportunities envisioned in the City's Official Community Plan;
WHEREAS clear application requirements, established processing timelines and accessible information about application status can improve the applicant experience, reduce incomplete and repeat submissions, and support greater efficiency and accountability in municipal service delivery;
WHEREAS municipalities that establish service standards, measure and publicly report on performance, and provide applicants with clear information about the progress of their applications can strengthen transparency, improve service delivery and build public confidence;
WHEREAS establishing measurable service standards and regularly reporting on performance will better equip Council to assess service levels, identify opportunities for improvement, make informed decisions about staffing, resources and fees, and mitigate the economic and reputational risks associated with unpredictable approval timelines;
THEREFORE BE IT RESOLVED THAT Council direct staff to report back by the end of the second quarter of 2027 with the following:
1. Current Processing Times: An assessment of current processing times for building permits and business licences, broken down by application category and type, establishing a baseline that measures both the total elapsed time from application submission to final decision and the time attributable to active City review, applicant responses and external agency requirements;
2. Proposed Service Standards: Recommended processing time standards for each permit and licence category, expressed in business days, weeks or months, with clear definitions of when processing timelines begin, pause and resume, including a same-day or next-business-day service standard for low-risk business licences and renewals that do not require inspections or referrals;
3. Clear Application Requirements: Plain-language application requirements for each permit and licence category, supported by accessible checklists and pre-application guidance, to ensure applicants understand what is required before submitting an application and to help reduce incomplete and repeat submissions;
4. Application Status Transparency: A proposed approach and implementation timeline to enable applicants to track the progress of their applications, including the current stage of review, outstanding requirements and the party responsible for addressing them, and an anticipated completion date, with proactive notification when delays occur;
5. Public Performance Reporting: A proposed framework and implementation timeline for publicly reporting the City's performance against established service standards on its website, at least quarterly, including both total elapsed application timelines and City processing times, with delays attributable to applicants or external agencies identified separately, to strengthen transparency, accountability and continuous service improvement;
6. Regulatory and Process Improvements: Recommended amendments, where necessary, to the Construction Regulation Bylaw, Business Licence Bylaw and applicable fee bylaws, together with measures to streamline processes, reduce incomplete and repeat applications, and support the achievement of established service standards;
AND THAT, following Council's adoption of service standards, staff report annually on the City's performance against those standards, including overall application timelines, the reasons for variances, opportunities for service improvement and recommended adjustments to ensure the standards remain effective, achievable and responsive to community needs.
